{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity","id":933863,"title":"Universal Humanity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-19T07:27:32-07:00","id":933863,"title":"Universal Humanity","content":"I just wanted to throw out a few ideas about how to universalize Humanity across the supernatural types (I will add universal rules for Virtues later, as they are a little more complicated, but assume that every supernatural creatures that can develop Humanity have the Virtues of Conscience, Courage, and Self-Control and that their base Humanity is equal to Conscience plus Self-Control while their base Willpower is equal to Courage). The problem with adding Humanity is that no one other than vampires gain anything from following Humanity, so I have created a few rules to encourage other supernatural creatures to keep their Humanity. Of course, it is up to every ST to decide whether they want to add Humanity to their non-vampire chronicles.<br /> <br /> Humanity Benefits:<br /> <br /> Changelings: If temporary Banality is less than Humanity, a changeling will lose an additional point of temporary Banality every day, to a minimum of '0'. If temporary Glamour is less than Humanity, a changeling will gain an additional point of temporary Glamour per day, up to their permanent Glamour.<br /> <br /> Demons: Demons may not take Humanity.<br /> <br /> Hunters: Hunters may not take Humanity because the process of Imbuement replaces their Humanity with Conviction.<br /> <br /> Mages: If temporary Paradox is less than Humanity, a mage will lose an additional point of temporary Paradox every day, to a minimum of '0'. Mages reduce their maximum Humanity by their permanent Paradox and, if their maximum Humanity is ever reduced to '0', they are ejected into the Umbra.<br /> <br /> Mortals: It temporary Willpower is less than Humanity, a mortal will gain an additional point of Willpower every day, up to a maximum of permanent Willpower.<br /> <br /> Mummies: Mummies do not have Humanity because they have a superior form of Humanity.<br /> <br /> Shapeshifters: If temporary Gnosis is less than Humanity, a shapeshifter will gain an additional point of Gnosis every day, up to a maximum of permanent Gnosis. Shapeshifters do not check for losing Humanity when they commit necessary Sins on behalf of Gaia (no cannibalism, rape, torture, etc.).<br /> <br /> Wraiths: If temporary Angst is less than Humanity, a wraith will lose an additional point of temporary Angst every day, to a minimum of '0'. Wraiths reduce their maximum Humanity by their permanent Angst and, if their maximum Humanity is ever reduced to '0', they become Specters (when using Humanity, Wraiths automatically begin with a permanent Angst of 1, though they may gain five extra bonus points for each additional level of permanent Angst that they take during character creation, up to their permanent Willpower).<br /> <br /> Vampires: Eastern Vampires may not take Humanity. Western Vampire receive their normal benefits from Humanity (in my games, I allow vampires who mimic humans to ignore daylight penalties and sunlight damage, and I allow vampires who maintain Humanity 8+ to produce Dhampir children, but that is just me).<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T22:27:35-07:00","id":934301,"title":"","content":"It seems kind of silly to make Humanity &quot;Universal,&quot; and then have four groups that don't use it.<br /> <br /> A lot of these bonuses make things far too easy on the &quot;good&quot; factions of their games. They'll have the most members with high Humanity, and these bonuses basically let them ignore some of the largest drawbacks they have to deal with. They have minimal reasons to engage in Humanity risky behavior, especially because they now gain so much for having high Humanity. Why is there even an Unseelie Court if being &quot;bad&quot; screws you so hard in comparison?<br /> <br /> The WtA caveat basically makes giving them Humanity pointless, since as long as their doing what the PCs are supposed to be going they can ignore the majority of the chart that would actually being interesting to struggle with.<br /> <br /> -------<br /> <br /> If you're going to go here, the best start is to not use VtM's systems as the starting point and use something that works better for a universal mechanic that can be tweaked for each game as needed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-19T23:40:16-07:00","id":934319,"title":"","content":"I get it, I really do--the urge to homogenize everything to the same consistency. Trust me, though, the differences between the groups are there for a reason.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1503-faradn","name":"Faradn","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1503_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"792"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T07:46:43-07:00","id":934490,"title":"","content":"The problem is that there are no consequences for evil behavior except for vampires. A mage can kill off a town of fifty thousand people as an experiment and nothing happens to them. A werewolf could rape and torture every family member of every Pentex employee and nothing happens to them. At least with Humanity, something happens to them if they are evil.<br /> <br /> The four groups you mention have a replacement for Humanity, similar to the Paths of the Sabbat (Balance for Mummies, Conviction for Hunters, Dharma for Kuei-jin, and Faith for Demons). As for good guys gaining the benefits, that it the point of Humanity. Bad guys get the cash and the girls, so good guys need something in compensation. It is not like there are any other form of karma rewards in WoD.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T08:39:41-07:00","id":934507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=934490#post934490\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that there are no consequences for evil behavior except for vampires.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends, but WoD Humanity is a crappy system to even start addressing this goal in the first place.<br /> <br /> Werewolves? The have mechanics for doing &quot;bad&quot; stuff in Renown and Rank. If you're bad, you lose Renown, and potentially Rank, which is bad for you. Doing the Wyrm's work by being a rape-happy monster is a fast way to getting killed off by your fellow Garou as a far more immediate concern anyway.<br /> <br /> Mages? Jhor is the biggest counter to traditionally bad stuff. Killing people with magic is supposed to be the fastest way to Jhor-town, Quiet Realms, and Paradox spirits.<br /> <br /> Changelings? Well they already have Banality as a &quot;you keep doing bad stuff so here's penalties&quot; thing, plus other things like Bedlam to worry about.<br /> <br /> Wraiths? They've got that whole, &quot;Shadow tempting me to become a spectre&quot; thing constantly on their backs without Humanity.<br /> <br /> So, no, I don't think the base-line premise that the games don't provide consequences is accurate in the first place. You don't need Humanity to do so; esp. not Humanity RAW from VtM.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The four groups you mention have a replacement for Humanity, similar to the Paths of the Sabbat (Balance for Mummies, Conviction for Hunters, Dharma for Kuei-jin, and Faith for Demons). As for good guys gaining the benefits, that it the point of Humanity. Bad guys get the cash and the girls, so good guys need something in compensation. It is not like there are any other form of karma rewards in WoD. </div> </div> </div>The idea that Conviction is a &quot;replacement&quot; for Humanity is... boggling. You don't lose Conviction for doing &quot;bad&quot; things. Depending on what you did, and your Creed, you can easily gain it by doing bad things.<br /> <br /> However, this highlights something that these games, and the CofD already figured out. Even if you want there to be a &quot;morality&quot; trait for each group, it only really works if it works differently for each group rather than trying to make them all fit one mold.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T09:06:49-07:00","id":934521,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Faradn</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=934319#post934319\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I get it, I really do--the urge to homogenize everything to the same consistency. Trust me, though, the differences between the groups are there for a reason.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. But more, The struggle to retain Humanity is a core conceit in Vampire. The whole &quot;Beast I am Lest Beast I Become&quot; or whatever. Vampire is supposed to be angsty. RPG angstploitation. It's a limitation that's worked into the fabric of the game even when the game is not dripping with self-flagellation in that it causes problems even when trying to function in those theaters of play which are less like a gothic Romance from the sympathetic villain's POV, like Vampire the Great Game (political) or super-heroes/villains with fangs (combat/mission oriented). It's even present when it's NOT present, aka the whole Paths thing to allow for vampires that weren't self-hating monsters and were more comfortable in their photo-adverse skins.<br /> <br /> But that's not Werewolf as written. Sure it COULD be. More Lon Chaney Jr than furry Captain Planet with anger management issues . But that would have made a game just like vampire with a different thing to be depressed about. I'm sure that had a lot to do with why Werewolf came out the way it did. Vampire 2 (only not... only, actually, yes) wouldn't have been much of a game. It would probably have worked better if Humanity had been important in Werewolf to have treated it as a supplement, like Hunters Hunted or Mummy were. <br /> <br /> And it's not Mage. Sure Mage can be about the struggle to remain &quot;human&quot; as a balance to hubris. But, geeze, that is going to SUCK for most of the different groups of mages out there. Humanity for Euthanatos? Verbena? Generically, these guys see ritual murder as a sacrament. What if you are a &quot;short path&quot; Cultist or Akashic? Victimizing people ca be a big part of your road to enlightenment. Hermetics? Resisting hubris is actually something they may talk about, but, really, the culture is all about the opposite. I mean I COULD see Humanity as an interesting concept in Mage and keep it fairly close to the game as is. But it would probably entail a Path of Enlightenment analogue for most of the groups (one for each Tradition and more for most Disparate groups.... though a few might get away with staying &quot;On the Path&quot; and, perhaps one for the whole Technocratic Union) and getting off of Humanity onto the appropriate alternative morality would be a key to power. Humanity would effectively be holding the mage back. Accepting the Path associated with the group in question would probably be THE key to achieving additional acumen in whatever uber-focus/praxis defining the group in question. Once again, not a bad game. In fact, it sounds pretty interesting to me personally, but not the game that's out there.<br /> <br /> If Hunters, Mummies, Demons and Kuei-jin already have their own replacement, how do the Wraiths not? The whole interplay between Angst and Pathos, Shadow and Psyche is about the most involved passion play of morality that you can possibly imagine.<br /> <br /> And as has been pointed out, making Changeting into Seelie Changeling loses that &quot;dark majesty/nightmare&quot; half of the game. It's less interesting and definitely much more like the &quot;pretty bubbles&quot; version of Changeling that gives the game a bad rep as it is.<br /> <br /> The &quot;Morality Stat&quot; per game works in the Chronicles of Darkness because the trajectory of each morality makes sense for the supernatural in question. It's built in. And, in some cases, the group is simpler. Requiem vamps don't have to deal with Sabbat-style vamps. All the mages are effectively the Houses of Hermes redone theosophist-style. And the sins can just be standard Morality + Sins Against Magic. Etc. Trying to add morality onto the World of Darkness games is going to be grafting something on that won't really fit with the feel or mechanics of the games in question. Or it's going to take some vivisection of the game in question and surgical messing about in its innards to make it work.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-20T11:03:10-07:00","id":934599,"title":"","content":"One of the reasons for universal Humanity is that everyone, except for Demons, were once mortals. Mortals have Humanity, it is part of being a mortal, and the fact that vampires can be more human than mortals is one of the themes of Vampire the Masquerade. If we start from that position, supernatural creatures that lack Humanity are more alien to mortals than even a low Humanity vampires, so it makes sense to have Humanity (or some replacement for Humanity) for each supernatural entity, so they can avoid alienating human beings. With the Kuei-jin, it is the higher of their Dharma or their Hun. With Mummies, Balance is a superior form of Humanity. With Hunters, Conviction overwrites Humanity just as the Hunter Virtues overwrite Mortal Virtues. With Demon, they are demons, so they have no need for Humanity, but you could imagine that their Faith overwrites the Humanity of their host. The other supernaturals, however, do not have sufficient equivalents (neither Banality or Glamour are a replacement for Humanity, neither Gnosis or Rage are a replacement for Humanity, etc).<br /> <br /> If you do not want to have crossovers with Vampire, you do not need to have Humanity in your games. However, like many of the people here, I have been playing White Wolf for over twenty years, and I would imagine that the majority of the people here have wanted better crossover rules. If I send Shih against a Hengeyokai group, the Hengeyokai group will probably appreciate that the reason why the Shih are not slaughtering their Kinfolk is because the Shih want to keep their Humanity. That is the advantage of Humanity is crossovers, it sets the guidelines that prevent one group from committing atrocities against another group and, when the rules are broken, there are consequences within the mechanics of the game as well as within the story of the game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T09:39:33-07:00","id":935099,"title":"","content":"Again, though, it can gut the central conceits of the other games. Werewolves in WtA DON'T CARE about the things that monkeys care about. Sure a newbie MIGHT for while, until s/he gets cuffed and growled at because all their maudlin wailing is getting in the way of doing their job saving the world. Most of them may have been born human (which begs the question of what your universal Humanity means to lupus), but they need to get over it. If they don't, they are a liability. And since the point of the game is fighting for Gaia against the End of Things, having mopey werewolves doesn't really jibe.<br /> <br /> A lot of the mages don't subscribe to the tenets of Humanity. Their magic and wordview don't consider those things important. They consider other things important instead. So ALL of those characters are going to be set at an inherent disadvantage. <br /> <br /> Etc. <br /> <br /> That's WHY there are the Paths in Vampire. Once the Sabbat became playable, there had to be some way to get around Humanity to make the game playable, as Sabbat characters would end up in Wassail pretty much de jure. <br /> <br /> So, if you do what you want you need to make up the &quot;other Moralities&quot; for all the relevant groups to make those games workable in this system. Or else, just say, Unseelie fae, Sabbat characters, a chunk of the Tradition mages, etc. are effectively unplayable after a very short time. <br /> <br /> And sure, there are probably some supernaturals out there that would have no problem killing a town of humans off to achieve their objective (like, say, a town possessed by Banes vomited out of a Hive) if they got in the way, wash the blood off and go out for drinks....","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T10:29:36-07:00","id":935124,"title":"","content":"What about Kinfolk, Kinain, Sorcerers, etc? Should not Kinfolk, Kinain, Sorcerers, etc be portrayed with Humanity and Mortal Virtues since they are human? Or are they just little psychopaths running around raping and killing without a care?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T11:14:10-07:00","id":935145,"title":"","content":"Why would you *need* a humanity rating for them?<br /> For a Vampire, adhering to (a VERY idealized) path of humanity is what keeps them from being overwhelmed by the beast. Unless they follow a Path, it is a necessity for them to not sink too low. An inherent part of being a Vampire on that road.<br /> <br /> To a Human/Kinfolk/Kinain/Sorcerer, that problem does not exist. It just doesn't.<br /> The pros and cons of 'behaving very human' (heh) are there, simply in the form of all the good and bad consequences of their actions. There is no mystical circumstance that rewards or punishes you for it.<br /> All a humanity system for their characters would do is, it would simplify the complex psychological and social reasons for behaving a certain way into 'You did a lot of bad stuff, you are insane now'. Or..something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 07-21-2016, 02:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T11:17:28-07:00","id":935150,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=935124#post935124\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What about Kinfolk, Kinain, Sorcerers, etc? Should not Kinfolk, Kinain, Sorcerers, etc be portrayed with Humanity and Mortal Virtues since they are human? Or are they just little psychopaths running around raping and killing without a care?</div> </div> </div> </div>They're from non Vampire games. Humanity as a trait is a central theme to vampire, not to the other games. If you are playing Vampire, then Kinfolk have a Humanity score. If you are playing Werewolf, then there is no need. That's really all it comes down to.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T11:27:27-07:00","id":935157,"title":"","content":"And if you are playing a crossover game?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T12:12:46-07:00","id":935184,"title":"","content":"Then it is up to you (or the ST) to determine what rule core set you guys are playing with and work out rules from there.<br /> <br /> I've run a crossover game where I kept track of everyone's Humanity ratings (all PCs started out as seemingly normal humans). I did it myself, for the non-vampire players, since it didn't add anything to their characters or to the themes of the game in regards to their characters.<br /> Humanity as a stat doesn't have any real meaning to anyone but vampires, and trying to interject it into the other games usually only serves to overcomplicate (or worse, take away from) the core themes of the other lines.<br /> This is obviously just me speaking from my experience with it. There is a chance I am in the minority and that no one else has had issues with humanity-driven Garou or Mages, etc.<br /> <br /> btw: all it seemed to do was lower the difficulty of some Vampire disciplines being used against the Garou PC. So for me, it was purely a mechanical effect or making the PC more susceptible to uncommon (from our game's standpoint) and specific powers.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-21T15:42:39-07:00","id":935309,"title":"","content":"I've been in a lot of crossover games.<br /> <br /> Humanity has only really mattered when Vampire rules assume targets have Humanity/Virtues when they don't (the other games are better about giving options for targets that don't have game specific traits like Rage), not when it came to RP or similar things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-22T21:41:42-07:00","id":935931,"title":"","content":"I'm kind of curious how &quot;it started human&quot; counts for animal breed/Metis changers, Inanimae and Adhere but not for Imbued Hunters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-27T08:44:23-07:00","id":938138,"title":"","content":"I have experimented with this idea of giving everyone humanity too. Though, the best solution I have found so far is to turn it on the head.<br /> Do not measure how Human the characters are, but how much sway their dark (and quite possible insane) side has gotten over them.<br /> <br /> In crossover-games I use &quot;Torment&quot; as an inverted humanity.<br /> The more Torment a character aquires, the deeper he/she is shaken in her grip on sanity and her own personality.<br /> <br /> For a normal human this is bad enough, causing PTSD, selfdoubt, depression and in severe cases even a downward-slide into madness.<br /> <br /> For supernatural beings with some kind of &quot;shadow&quot; as I call it collectively (The name-giving shadow of wraith, the beast of vampires, the forgotten demon in demon the fallen, the rage in werewolf, the demon-soul in kuey-jin, the bane inside a fomory etc...) it gets worse. In this cases the more Torment you aquire the stronger this &quot;shadow&quot; gets, and the more hold it has over your character, coloring its thoughts and actions with its own take on reality.<br /> <br /> Implementing it is rather easy. Demon the fallen already uses this stat, so no need for conversion here. In Wraith you simply swap out the termes &quot;Angst&quot; for &quot;Torment&quot; and you are good to go. For Vampire you take 10, subtract humanity, and what is left is your Torment-rating. The rest simply starts at 3 Torment (or you may use virtues in your char-gen for them too, then its calculated the same way as for vampires).<br /> <br /> As for raising and lowering Torment, it is a little more intuitive than humanity. Simply ask yourself, is this an action the character would regrett enough, or is so shocking, that it would cause her nightmares, great lingering regrett or jade him/her from the brutality of the world?<br /> If the answer is yes, then you have a roll on your hands.<br /> <br /> Likewise to lower Torment you ask yourself, if an action would allow your character to shake off some of his lingering angst, if it could heal whatever wound was left in his heart or soul, or allow him/her a better grip on her own self and sanity. If yes, then there is the chance of lowering his/her Torment.<br /> <br /> I've found, that this lends itself much smother to a wide variety of moralitys than a rigid system like humanity, while providing the same benefits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6112,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6112-seki","user":"Seki","date":". 07-27-2016, 11:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6112-seki","name":"Seki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-27T10:35:52-07:00","id":938194,"title":"","content":"Rage is not a bad thing in Werewolf, though it can be a dangerous thing, and it is not the Other of the Changing Breeds (unlike the P'o, Shadow, etc). It is the righteous anger of the Changing Breeds against the enemies of Gaia. A Garou with a Rage of 5 is not less human (or humane) than a Garou with a Rage of 1. A Rage 5 Garou is not more likely to willingly commit rape than a Rage 1 Garou. In addition permanent Rage rarely ever decreases and, when it does, it is considered to be a bad thing.<br /> <br /> One problem with the Torment system that you describe that it does not allow for the Paths of Enlightenment. It also does not capture the true nature of the Kuei-jin, as effective Humanity is the greater of Dharma or Hun (P'o is not part of the equation). In addition, it should be harder to lose Torment than it is to gain it, as the effects of evil actions often outweigh the effects of good actions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T11:17:46-07:00","id":938965,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=938194#post938194\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Rage is not a bad thing in Werewolf, though it can be a dangerous thing, and it is not the Other of the Changing Breeds (unlike the P'o, Shadow, etc). It is the righteous anger of the Changing Breeds against the enemies of Gaia. A Garou with a Rage of 5 is not less human (or humane) than a Garou with a Rage of 1. A Rage 5 Garou is not more likely to willingly commit rape than a Rage 1 Garou. In addition permanent Rage rarely ever decreases and, when it does, it is considered to be a bad thing.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are correct, that rage is not only a bad thing for a Garou, it can be very usefull. But it is also extremely dangerous for them and a constant struggle to keep in check and directed against their enemys instead against anyone they truely care about<b></b>:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> This Rage is a force of mindless violence, a<br /> constant slow-motion path of unthinking destruction and<br /> violence that each and every Garou must keep control of lest<br /> it consume her. Rage mixes instinctual cunning and hunting<br /> ability with savage bloodlust and unpredictable horror.<br /> To the Garou, Rage is both a blessing and a curse.<br /> Many think that Luna gave Rage to the Garou through<br /> her moon-signs that determine how much Rage a Garou<br /> begins his life with after the First Change. Others contend<br /> that Rage is a spark of the Wyrm within each Garou, the<br /> force of primal destruction corrupting the children of the<br /> creator. The most vocal say that it is Gaia herself who would<br /> have her children use her Rage as their greatest weapon.<br /> Much of a Garou\u2019s struggle comes from a never-ending<br /> battle with the Rage each werewolf feels. The Beast is<br /> never far from their thoughts \u2014 even the most pacifistic<br /> Ragabash or the most serene of the Children of Gaia looks<br /> at a normal human and must repress the urge to rend and<br /> tear and bite until all that\u2019s left is blood and meat. Ahroun<br /> have it far worse, fighting to see friends, family, and loved<br /> ones as little more than prey animals or targets for attack.<br /> <br /> W20 page 146 </div> </div> </div>This is the struggle a werewolf must face, when his rage starts to take a bigger and bigger role in his personality. I never said, that a high-torment Werewolf would be more ready to rape someone than a low-torment one (even though it actually would be true for high rage metis, but this is besides the point), instead the high torment would manifest in different ways. It would make him both spiritualy troubled, vulnerable to Harano, and difficult for him to surpress his rage, when not appropriate.<br /> <br /> As the core-rulebook states, he would have growing difficulty seeing anyone he sees as anything other than prey or foes to be destroyed. This would grow with growing Torment. He would have growing difficulty to get his thoughts on other things then violence, and would start to regard violence as the go-to solution to his problems, even if other paths could potentialy be more helpfull. His inner beast would gain ever more influence over him, urging him to act more and more like an apex-predator in every situation, and to ruthlessly fight to gain whatever he wants.<br /> <br /> There are two ways this would usually go, either he grows to despise what he's become, trying to distance him from it and his rage, bringing him on a path close to Harano.<br /> Or he could come to love and embrace it, becoming ever more beast-like, till he loses himself to his anger and base instincts like a 0-Humanity Vampire potentionally could.<br /> <br /> If he commites rape himself, for whatever reason, this would certainly not be helpfull for his mental health or control over his inner beast, and would most likely let him slip deeper into Torment and unrestraint, but High-Torment itself would not propell him to do such a thing.<br /> <br /> Also, high Rage would not be the same as high Torment. His Rage-rating allows him to tap into his primal Rage to fuel him in various helpfull ways. His Torment-Rating would reflect how well he has it under control and how far his Rage holds sway and influence over his mind and personality.<br /> <br /> For the Kuei-jin it would be similar. If my memory serves me right, Dharma is more akin to spiritual enlightenment, having little to do with Humanity. Not sure about Hun though. For them Torment would work in a similar way, as for those on a path of Enlightenment, that you mentioned.<br /> They have differing belive- and morality-systems, than a normal human.<br /> <br /> That does not change, that they can feel conflicted or remorsfull about things however. It just changes, what actions would trigger such conflict or remorse.<br /> Torment is a measurement of all those lingering remorse, self-hate, indecision and internal conflict, that allow his/her dark side to sway him and underminde his grip on sanity.<br /> So gaining Torment could potentialy result from radical different actions depending on personality, personal believe and moral-code of the character and society around them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":6112,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6112-seki","user":"Seki","date":". 07-28-2016, 02:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6112-seki","name":"Seki","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"51"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T11:34:18-07:00","id":938974,"title":"","content":"No, a Werewolf will never be as angry as a Humanity 0 Vampire. A Humanity 0 Vampire is a creature of no intellect. Anyway, I see Torment as a trait that represents the inhumanity of a Demon, a creature that was never human, so it should never be used for a creature that was once human, like a Vampire, or a creature that is a spiritual hybrid of human and animal, like a Werewolf (even a lupus is more human than a Demon because they can inherently transform into a human rather than raping the soul of a mortal to gain control of a body). <br /> <br /> If you introduced Humanity to Werewolf, the advantage would be that high Humanity Werewolves would be more capable of controlling their Rage because their habit of acting human would keep them from losing control of their emotions. One possible mechanic would be to allow Werewolves to make a Humanity roll (difficulty equal to the Rage) to check to see if they frenzy (instead of the normal Rage roll). If they fail, they frenzy, and may be required to make a Conscience roll if they committed a sin against Humanity. If they botch, they go under the Thrall of the Wyrm, automatically lose a level of Humanity, and will be required to make an Conscience roll to avoid losing another level of Humanity. They would be protected somewhat by the love of Gaia, one of the perks of being one of the Changing Breeds allied to Gaia, which would prevent them from being required to check for Humanity loss when they destroy the property of organizations allied to the Wyrm or kill agents who are knowingly working for the Wyrm, as long as it is done in the defense of Gaia.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T12:56:30-07:00","id":939057,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=938974#post938974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> No, a Werewolf will never be as angry as a Humanity 0 Vampire. A Humanity 0 Vampire is a creature of no intellect.</div> </div> </div> </div>Both of these statements seem wrong to me. Humanity 0 Vampires aren't angry per se. They're bestial which is completely different to me. And they certainly have intellect. Wights can and should be cunning. And I don't think they take any sort of Intelligence penalty so they aren't stupid by any means. It just strikes me as odd to say they have no intellect.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=938974#post938974\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> If you introduced Humanity to Werewolf, the advantage would be that high Humanity Werewolves would be more capable of controlling their Rage because their habit of acting human would keep them from losing control of their emotions. One possible mechanic would be to allow Werewolves to make a Humanity roll (difficulty equal to the Rage) to check to see if they frenzy (instead of the normal Rage roll). If they fail, they frenzy, and may be required to make a Conscience roll if they committed a sin against Humanity. If they botch, they go under the Thrall of the Wyrm, automatically lose a level of Humanity, and will be required to make an Conscience roll to avoid losing another level of Humanity. They would be protected somewhat by the love of Gaia, one of the perks of being one of the Changing Breeds allied to Gaia, which would prevent them from being required to check for Humanity loss when they destroy the property of organizations allied to the Wyrm or kill agents who are knowingly working for the Wyrm, as long as it is done in the defense of Gaia.</div> </div> </div> </div>Starting by saying I don't see the point of even trying to add Humanity to Werewolf. If for some reason though you were to insist upon it, this premise of this system seems solid enough, even if I disagree with the exact mechanics of it.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T13:44:37-07:00","id":939101,"title":"","content":"Wights are savage animals, they have animal cunning, not human intellect. That seems to be one of the universal rules. If they are not hungry, they will pursue their Natures with animal cunning (a Caregiver may look over their former friends and family, though they would not be able to understand the subtleties of human interactions, and may interpret play fighting as real aggression; an Architect may create strangely inhuman buildings out of found items; a Scientist may dissect living creatures and examine their organs, etc). Their Willpower determines how many of their previous Ability levels they can retain beyond those required for base survival, but they will never be intellectual Abilities like Computer or Science.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T13:47:30-07:00","id":939103,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Seki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=938965#post938965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You are correct, that rage is not only a bad thing for a Garou, it can be very usefull. But it is also extremely dangerous for them and a constant struggle to keep in check and directed against their enemys instead against anyone they truely care about<b></b>:<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This is the struggle a werewolf must face, when his rage starts to take a bigger and bigger role in his personality. I never said, that a high-torment Werewolf would be more ready to rape someone than a low-torment one (even though it actually would be true for high rage metis, but this is besides the point), instead the high torment would manifest in different ways. It would make him both spiritualy troubled, vulnerable to Harano, and difficult for him to surpress his rage, when not appropriate.<br /> <br /> As the core-rulebook states, he would have growing difficulty seeing anyone he sees as anything other than prey or foes to be destroyed. This would grow with growing Torment. He would have growing difficulty to get his thoughts on other things then violence, and would start to regard violence as the go-to solution to his problems, even if other paths could potentialy be more helpfull. His inner beast would gain ever more influence over him, urging him to act more and more like an apex-predator in every situation, and to ruthlessly fight to gain whatever he wants.<br /> <br /> There are two ways this would usually go, either he grows to despise what he's become, trying to distance him from it and his rage, bringing him on a path close to Harano.<br /> Or he could come to love and embrace it, becoming ever more beast-like, till he loses himself to his anger and base instincts like a 0-Humanity Vampire potentionally could.<br /> <br /> If he commites rape himself, for whatever reason, this would certainly not be helpfull for his mental health or control over his inner beast, and would most likely let him slip deeper into Torment and unrestraint, but High-Torment itself would not propell him to do such a thing.<br /> <br /> Also, high Rage would not be the same as high Torment. His Rage-rating allows him to tap into his primal Rage to fuel him in various helpfull ways. His Torment-Rating would reflect how well he has it under control and how far his Rage holds sway and influence over his mind and personality.</div> </div> </div> </div>Best not to have universal Traits, but to have formulas to figure out what to roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> For the Kuei-jin it would be similar. If my memory serves me right, Dharma is more akin to spiritual enlightenment, having little to do with Humanity. Not sure about Hun though. For them Torment would work in a similar way, as for those on a path of Enlightenment, that you mentioned.<br /> They have differing belive- and morality-systems, than a normal human. </div> </div> </div>Dharma generally gets used as a fill-in for a power stat, with Hun taking the mechanical role of Humanity. They aren't the same Traits, but the only Traits that need to be universal are Attributes, Abilities, and a few Backgrounds. Once you get into the monster bits, things stop being so clear cut and basic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That does not change, that they can feel conflicted or remorsfull about things however. It just changes, what actions would trigger such conflict or remorse.<br /> Torment is a measurement of all those lingering remorse, self-hate, indecision and internal conflict, that allow his/her dark side to sway him and underminde his grip on sanity.<br /> So gaining Torment could potentialy result from radical different actions depending on personality, personal believe and moral-code of the character and society around them. </div> </div> </div>Torment is for a game that doesn't feature Garou. Werewolf has to deal with the possibility of Harano, battle scars, Thrall of the Wyrm, frenzy rolls triggered by everyday stimuli, running out of Rage (as bad as constantly being awash in it, if not worse) and losing the wolf. Their powers are also a bitch and a half to acquire. And on top of all of that, the only changers that aren't inherently short-lived and mortal are the Rokea, who also contend with having a very hard time keeping up relations with the human half.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T14:16:36-07:00","id":939128,"title":"","content":"The Gurahl are less vulnerable to their Rage, so they can actually have quite high levels of Rage and still be functional in polite society, though most humans will feel that there is something wrong with them and will avoid them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-28T14:48:38-07:00","id":939156,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/933863-universal-humanity?p=939128#post939128\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Gurahl are less vulnerable to their Rage, so they can actually have quite high levels of Rage and still be functional in polite society, though most humans will feel that there is something wrong with them and will avoid them.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's a function of Rage compared to Willpower, mostly. It does take them more successes to frenzy, but when it comes to social rolls, Rage 10 Gurahl with no extra Willpower and a Rank too low to get the curse-alleviating Gift (which is an elder power) will need to be the world's best socialites just to get the results of the well-mannered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}